<!--
    kuna issue testcase: an x86-32 callee's own code bounds the stack arguments
    a call passes it (`option calleeargs on|off`, default-ON).

    Issue:      kuna GH-1562 - calls in a function that builds a by-value
                struct argument lose their pushed arguments, and a push for an
                outer call is credited to an inner this-only call.
    Stage:      P4 calls - checkInputTrialUse's stack trials
                (`p4_calls/kuna_calleestackargs.rs`, `funcdata_callsite.rs`).
    P0 assert:  `option calleeargs on|off`.

    Each fixture is decompiled twice: `option calleeargs off`, then the
    shipped default.
    noargs_pe_i386.exe (VC8 /O2, notes in noargs_pe_i386.txt): steer pushes
    `extra` for a virtual call, then calls the this-only Vec::angle (whose ECX
    `this` may print as its argument). Off, angle takes `extra`; on, the
    virtual call gets it as its fourth argument.
    epilogcleanup_pe_i386.exe (source epilogcleanup_pe_i386.s): the same
    shape in a function with a 16-byte local frame, so `pop edi; pop esi;
    add esp,0x10` follows the virtual call. The cleanup scan after angle
    takes the virtual call to pop only its return address and counted the
    frame teardown as angle's cleanup; it now stops at the epilogue. Its
    consts defers a cdecl cleanup past `push 3; pop ebx` (MSVC /O1's
    `mov ebx,3`): that pop is not the epilogue, and the call keeps both.
    byval_pe_i386.exe (notes in byval_pe_i386.txt): draw calls paint(a, b, 3)
    before a `mov edi,esp; rep movsd` by-value copy. The copy is expanded
    before the stack is heritaged (kuna-repframecopy.xml), so paint keeps all
    three in both passes.
    repframecopy_pe_i386.exe, big_draw (0x401240): paint3(a, b, 3) before a
    32-dword by-value copy, too large to expand. Off, paint3 prints with no
    arguments; on, with all three.
    stdcallpop_pe_i386.exe, the CRT _NMSG_WRITE (0x40264d): off, strcpy, strcat
    and strlen print with no arguments; on, each with its own, and strlen with
    exactly one. Both passes pin that GetStdHandle and WriteFile take no
    argument from the CRT calls before them.
    cdecllocal_pe_i386.exe (VC8 /O2, notes in cdecllocal_pe_i386.txt): count
    stores `x = 0x1234` beside the push for the cdecl tally(c) and passes &x
    later. Neither pass gives tally the local as a second argument.
    Last, stdcallpop's 0x4027a0 calls a helper that reads one byte of its third
    argument; the slot still counts whole, so the call keeps all three.
    calleeframes_pe_i386.exe (clean-room asm, notes in
    calleeframes_pe_i386.asm), start (0x401200), both passes: seh_open reads
    its arguments through the EBP the CRT-shaped seh_prolog4 helper set, so its
    extent is not stated and it keeps both; map_string calls the alloca helper;
    use_add2 reads its third argument past a push; log_msg hands va_start's
    address to vsprintf; pick reads its second argument through a copy of ESP.
    Every call keeps every argument it is passed.
    The _NMSG_WRITE tail: GetStdHandle and WriteFile are called through the
    import table, and the win32sigs parameters parked at their slots reach
    both calls in both passes, because the caller's pushes back every declared
    slot: WriteFile's last three arguments are pushed before strlen (whose own
    return pops only its return address) and GetStdHandle (whose declaration
    states its pop) are called. So the push of -12 is GetStdHandle's one
    argument and WriteFile takes it and its other four (#25). Before those
    parameters reached a call through the slot, GetStdHandle was unprototyped
    and refused every stack trial by the upstream local-alias test (the
    function takes its own parameter's address): off, the push stayed a local
    store; on, it was dead and the constant was gone.
    calleelost_pe_i386.exe (clean-room asm, notes in calleelost_pe_i386.asm),
    both passes: call_ret (0x401050), call_xmm (0x401070) and call_pushesp
    (0x401090) pass (0x11, 0x22) to callees that read the second argument
    through a stack address a call returns, one parked in xmm0, or one stored
    and loaded back; none of those reads can be placed, so each call keeps both.
    cookie_caller (0x4010d0) pushes a cookie-style local, then calls the bounded
    keep_one and pass_on, whose callee states no extent: the local is not
    dropped from keep_one where pass_on would take it, so pass_on keeps its one
    argument.
    call_argslot (0x401190), call_movq (0x4011b0) and call_ecx (0x4011d0), both
    passes, pass (0x11, 0x22) to callees that read the second argument through a
    local's address stored over their first argument, one copied by an 8-byte
    movq, or a stack address a call leaves in ECX; each call keeps both, and so
    does call_heap (0x401230), whose callee stores its stack pointer through a
    pointer it loaded and reads past it after loading it back. The
    option-off session ends by turning the option on and decompiling call_ret2
    (0x4011f0), a second caller of the get_sp reader, whose callee was already
    walked with the option off: it keeps both too.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
